[{:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-10-28T21:56:42.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-10-28T21:56:42.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://faster-than-light-memes.xyz/my-core-logic-fork_en.html", :title "My core.logic fork", :link "https://faster-than-light-memes.xyz/my-core-logic-fork_en.html", :published-at #inst "2026-10-28T23:59:59.000-00:00", :content "My core.logic fork ftlm :faster-than-light /memes Joyful ideas are better ideas search This is your fathers rock. I mean this is the searchbar. Work With Benjamin Hire me Resume CV Claude Code Programming with Claude Code Explore! Get a random piece of Bennis mind! Lisp What does it mean for a language to be beautiful? Documentaries etc. History of cybernetics and related fields FAQ Answers and reasons for everything. Books Like a whetstone is to a sword Posts A list of all posts. Screencasts Dancing My core.logic fork A series on making Clojure's maps, vectors and sets relational in core.logic: every relation runs in every direction, and anything it can't decide yet, it waits on visibly instead of guessing. The code and an overview are in the repository README. What it looks like Each relation is a Clojure function read as a relation, so any argument can be the unknown one. Maps. get, assoc, merge, conj and keys, forwards and backwards: (run* [v] (geto {:a 1 :b 2} :b v))          ;;=> (2)\n(run* [k] (geto {:a 1 :b 2} k 2))           ;;=> (:b)          which key holds 2\n\n(run* [m] (assoco m :b 2 {:a 1 :b 2}))\n;;=> ({:a 1} {:a 1, :b _0})                 ;; m had no :b, or any :b that assoc overwrote\n\n(run* [m1 m2] (mergeo m1 m2 {:a 1}))\n;;=> ([{:a 1} {}] [{} {:a 1}] [{:a _0} {:a 1}])\n\n(run* [q] (fresh [v] (conjo q [:foo v] {:foo 1})))\n;;=> ({} {:foo _0})                         ;; the wiki's test case, below\n\n(run* [k] (== {:a 1 k 2} {:a 1 :b 2}))      ;;=> (:b)          unknown keys unify\n(run* [ks] (keyso {:a 1 :b 2} ks))          ;;=> (#{:b :a})\n\n(run* [m] (geto m :a 1) (geto m :b 2))\n;;=> ((lmap {:b 2, :a 1} | _0))             ;; an open map: these keys, maybe more\n Vectors. nth, conj, concat, subvec, with lengths solved exactly: (run* [i] (ntho [:a :b :a] i :a))           ;;=> (0 2)\n(run* [v x] (conjo v x [1 2 3]))            ;;=> ([[1 2] 3])\n(run* [s e] (subveco [:a :b :c :d] s e [:b :c]))\n;;=> ([1 3])                                ;; where the slice came from\n\n(run* [a b] (concato a b [1 2 3]))\n;;=> ([[] [1 2 3]] [[1] [2 3]] [[1 2] [3]] [[1 2 3] []])\n\n(run* [v] (counto v 2) (ntho v 0 :a))       ;;=> ([:a _0])\n\n(run* [x] (fresh [l r] (concato x [1] l) (concato [1] x r) (concato r [2] l)))\n;;=> ()                                     ;; |x| + 1 ≠ |x| + 2, no search needed\n Sets. Literals with unknown elements, conj, union, difference: (run* [x y] (== #{x y} #{1 2}))             ;;=> ([1 2] [2 1])\n(run* [s] (conjo s 3 #{1 2 3}))             ;;=> (#{1 3 2} #{1 2})\n(run* [a] (uniono a #{2 3} #{1 2 3}))       ;;=> (#{1 3} #{1} #{1 3 2} #{1 2})\n(run* [x] (subseto #{1 x} #{1 2 3}))        ;;=> (3 2)         x is distinct from 1\n\n(run* [x] (differenceo #{1 2 3} x #{1}))\n;;=> (((lset #{3 2} | _0) :- (clojure.core.logic/lackso _0 1)))\n;; 2 and 3, plus a rest without 1\n\n(run* [s] (containso s 1) (containso s 2))\n;;=> ((lset #{1 2} | _0))\n Together. The relations compose across kinds, and paths are relations too: (def db {:users [{:name \"ada\" :tags #{:admin}} {:name \"bob\" :tags #{}}]})\n\n(run* [n] (fresh [us u tags]\n            (geto db :users us) (ntho us (lvar) u)\n            (geto u :name n) (geto u :tags tags) (containso tags :admin)))\n;;=> (\"ada\")\n\n(run* [p] (get-ino db p \"bob\"))             ;;=> ((:users 1 :name))\n\n;; the same question as a pattern: at least these keys, at least this element\n(run* [n] (fresh [i u] (get-ino db [:users i] u) (matcho u {:name n :tags #{:admin}})))\n;;=> (\"ada\")\n Why this is the core.logic thing A logic language takes its terms from its host. miniKanren's term is the Scheme pair, because Scheme's data is the pair: appendo and membero work so well because a partly known list has a natural shape, a known front and an unbound tail. core.logic brought miniKanren to Clojure, and the pair came along. Clojure's own data came in second class: two maps unified only if they had exactly the same keys, and apart from featurec reading values at known keys, nothing could take a map apart or build one. But the data is what Clojure is. Literal, immutable maps, vectors and sets, used for everything: \"just use maps\", data over functions over macros, a program is a pipeline of get, assoc, merge, conj and union. Clojure programmers don't encode a record as an association list or a set as a list with no duplicates; they write {:name \"ada\"} and #{1 2}. So a relational Clojure should be relational over that. This series makes it so: get, assoc, merge, nth, concat, union and friends, each as a relation that runs backwards. A query can ask which map an assoc came from, which key path leads to a value, or which two sets have this union, on the same data the rest of the program uses. That's the part of core.logic only Clojure can have, and what miniKanren-on-lists can only encode. This isn't a new wish. In 2013 David Nolen put it on core.logic's own roadmap. The wiki page Constraints & modes opens: \"core.logic unfortunately to this day continues to emphasize operating on sequence-like data structures due to its Prolog & Scheme roots\", and asks for \"constraint versions of Clojure functions\". It sketches an assocc, finds that running it backwards branches, and settles on conjo as the test case: (run* [q]\n  (fresh [v o]\n    (== o {:foo 1})\n    (conjo q [:foo v] o)))\n \"So we should see the following results: ({} {:foo _0}).\" That is exactly what the fork answers. The companion page CLP(Set) asks for sets with unknown elements, (unionc #{:cat x y} #{:dog :bird z} o), and notes that such a union branches over which elements pair up. The fork runs that one too: with o = #{:cat :dog :bird}, x and y are :dog and :bird in either order and z is :cat. (Writing this found a bug: the fork used to let x be :cat too. Fixed.) Both pages are still on the wiki's roadmap, under \"Immediate\", and were never built. The machinery they sketch is not what this series builds (a defc with mode declarations, set domains like the FD domains), but their test cases are what it answers. It also sets the bar. A relation that only works in some directions is a function with extra steps, so the series holds every relation to one rule: it answers correctly in every mode, or waits until it can (part 2). Relational maps: a tour of the API. No unsupported behaviour: why the design had to be redone, and how unknown keys, lazy checks and suspended goals fit together. Making it fast: from 33 s to 8 ms, and what was slow. Vectors: open vectors, lengths solved exactly, and get-in as a query language. Literature and miniKanren parity: where this sits in the research, and what core.logic still lacks. Towards relational hyperdimensional computing: the same data structures, encoded as hypervectors. Sets: maps without values, and what that buys. Side by side with miniKanren: faster-miniKanren's test suite run on core.logic, the bugs it found, and maps, sets and vectors next to their miniKanren encodings. A relational Clojure interpreter: evalo for a Clojure subset, with relational maps as environments; what it runs backwards, and where its search is still slow. Next, maybe: records as types, and ClojureScript. See future work. Generated by Claude on Benjamin Schwerdtner's behalf. Date: 2026-10-28 Wed 00:00 Email: Benjamin.Schwerdtner@gmail.com About Contact"}, :excerpts [{:text "... searchbar. Work With Benjamin Hire me Resume CV Claude Code Programming with Claude Code Explore! Get a random piece of Bennis mind!...", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://faster-than-light-memes.xyz/my-core-logic-fork_de.html", :title "Mein core.logic-Fork", :link "https://faster-than-light-memes.xyz/my-core-logic-fork_de.html", :published-at #inst "2026-10-28T23:59:59.000-00:00", :content "Mein core.logic-Fork ftlm :faster-than-light /memes Joyful ideas are better ideas search This is your fathers rock. I mean this is the searchbar. Work With Benjamin Hire me Resume CV Claude Code Programming with Claude Code Explore! Get a random piece of Bennis mind! Lisp What does it mean for a language to be beautiful? Documentaries etc. History of cybernetics and related fields FAQ Answers and reasons for everything. Books Like a whetstone is to a sword Posts A list of all posts. Screencasts Dancing Mein core.logic-Fork Eine Serie darüber, wie Clojures Maps, Vektoren und Sets in core.logic relational werden: Jede Relation läuft in jede Richtung, und was sie noch nicht entscheiden kann, darauf wartet sie sichtbar, statt zu raten. Code und Überblick stehen im README des Repositorys. Wie es aussieht Jede Relation ist eine Clojure-Funktion, als Relation gelesen, also kann jedes Argument das unbekannte sein. Maps. get, assoc, merge, conj und keys, vorwärts und rückwärts: (run* [v] (geto {:a 1 :b 2} :b v))          ;;=> (2)\n(run* [k] (geto {:a 1 :b 2} k 2))           ;;=> (:b)          welcher Key hat 2\n\n(run* [m] (assoco m :b 2 {:a 1 :b 2}))\n;;=> ({:a 1} {:a 1, :b _0})                 ;; m hatte kein :b, oder ein :b, das assoc überschrieb\n\n(run* [m1 m2] (mergeo m1 m2 {:a 1}))\n;;=> ([{:a 1} {}] [{} {:a 1}] [{:a _0} {:a 1}])\n\n(run* [q] (fresh [v] (conjo q [:foo v] {:foo 1})))\n;;=> ({} {:foo _0})                         ;; der Testfall aus dem Wiki, unten\n\n(run* [k] (== {:a 1 k 2} {:a 1 :b 2}))      ;;=> (:b)          unbekannte Keys unifizieren\n(run* [ks] (keyso {:a 1 :b 2} ks))          ;;=> (#{:b :a})\n\n(run* [m] (geto m :a 1) (geto m :b 2))\n;;=> ((lmap {:b 2, :a 1} | _0))             ;; eine offene Map: diese Keys, vielleicht mehr\n Vektoren. nth, conj, concat, subvec, mit exakt gelösten Längen: (run* [i] (ntho [:a :b :a] i :a))           ;;=> (0 2)\n(run* [v x] (conjo v x [1 2 3]))            ;;=> ([[1 2] 3])\n(run* [s e] (subveco [:a :b :c :d] s e [:b :c]))\n;;=> ([1 3])                                ;; woher der Ausschnitt stammt\n\n(run* [a b] (concato a b [1 2 3]))\n;;=> ([[] [1 2 3]] [[1] [2 3]] [[1 2] [3]] [[1 2 3] []])\n\n(run* [v] (counto v 2) (ntho v 0 :a))       ;;=> ([:a _0])\n\n(run* [x] (fresh [l r] (concato x [1] l) (concato [1] x r) (concato r [2] l)))\n;;=> ()                                     ;; |x| + 1 ≠ |x| + 2, ohne Suche\n Sets. Literale mit unbekannten Elementen, conj, union, difference: (run* [x y] (== #{x y} #{1 2}))             ;;=> ([1 2] [2 1])\n(run* [s] (conjo s 3 #{1 2 3}))             ;;=> (#{1 3 2} #{1 2})\n(run* [a] (uniono a #{2 3} #{1 2 3}))       ;;=> (#{1 3} #{1} #{1 3 2} #{1 2})\n(run* [x] (subseto #{1 x} #{1 2 3}))        ;;=> (3 2)         x ist verschieden von 1\n\n(run* [x] (differenceo #{1 2 3} x #{1}))\n;;=> (((lset #{3 2} | _0) :- (clojure.core.logic/lackso _0 1)))\n;; 2 und 3, plus ein Rest ohne 1\n\n(run* [s] (containso s 1) (containso s 2))\n;;=> ((lset #{1 2} | _0))\n Zusammen. Die Relationen lassen sich über die Arten hinweg kombinieren, und auch Pfade sind Relationen: (def db {:users [{:name \"ada\" :tags #{:admin}} {:name \"bob\" :tags #{}}]})\n\n(run* [n] (fresh [us u tags]\n            (geto db :users us) (ntho us (lvar) u)\n            (geto u :name n) (geto u :tags tags) (containso tags :admin)))\n;;=> (\"ada\")\n\n(run* [p] (get-ino db p \"bob\"))             ;;=> ((:users 1 :name))\n\n;; dieselbe Frage als Muster: mindestens diese Keys, mindestens dieses Element\n(run* [n] (fresh [i u] (get-ino db [:users i] u) (matcho u {:name n :tags #{:admin}})))\n;;=> (\"ada\")\n Warum das die eigentliche Sache von core.logic ist Eine Logiksprache nimmt ihre Terme von ihrer Wirtssprache. Der Term von miniKanren ist das Scheme-Paar, weil Schemes Daten das Paar sind: appendo und membero funktionieren so gut, weil eine teilweise bekannte Liste eine natürliche Form hat, einen bekannten Anfang und einen ungebundenen Rest. core.logic hat miniKanren nach Clojure gebracht, und das Paar kam mit. Clojures eigene Daten waren Bürger zweiter Klasse: Zwei Maps unifizierten nur, wenn sie genau dieselben Keys hatten, und außer featurec, das Werte an bekannten Keys liest, konnte nichts eine Map zerlegen oder eine bauen. Aber die Daten sind das, was Clojure ausmacht. Literale, unveränderliche Maps, Vektoren und Sets, für alles benutzt: \"just use maps\", Daten vor Funktionen vor Makros, ein Programm ist eine Pipeline aus get, assoc, merge, conj und union. Clojure-Leute kodieren einen Datensatz nicht als Assoziationsliste oder ein Set als Liste ohne Duplikate; sie schreiben {:name \"ada\"} und #{1 2}. Ein relationales Clojure sollte also über diese Daten relational sein. Diese Serie macht das wahr: get, assoc, merge, nth, concat, union und Verwandte, jeweils als Relation, die rückwärts läuft. Eine Anfrage kann fragen, aus welcher Map ein assoc kam, welcher Key-Pfad zu einem Wert führt oder welche zwei Sets diese Vereinigung haben, auf denselben Daten, die der Rest des Programms benutzt. Das ist der Teil von core.logic, den nur Clojure haben kann, und den miniKanren auf Listen nur kodieren kann. Der Wunsch ist nicht neu. 2013 hat David Nolen ihn auf die Roadmap von core.logic selbst gesetzt. Die Wiki-Seite Constraints & modes beginnt mit: \"core.logic unfortunately to this day continues to emphasize operating on sequence-like data structures due to its Prolog & Scheme roots\", und wünscht sich \"constraint versions of Clojure functions\". Sie skizziert ein assocc, stellt fest, dass es rückwärts verzweigt, und nimmt conjo als Testfall: (run* [q]\n  (fresh [v o]\n    (== o {:foo 1})\n    (conjo q [:foo v] o)))\n \"So we should see the following results: ({} {:foo _0}).\" Genau das antwortet der Fork. Die Begleitseite CLP(Set) wünscht sich Sets mit unbekannten Elementen, (unionc #{:cat x y} #{:dog :bird z} o), und merkt an, dass so eine Vereinigung darüber verzweigt, welche Elemente zusammenfallen. Auch die läuft im Fork: Mit o = #{:cat :dog :bird} sind x und y in beliebiger Reihenfolge :dog und :bird, und z ist :cat. (Beim Schreiben habe ich dabei einen Bug gefunden: Der Fork hat x früher auch :cat sein lassen. Behoben.) Beide Seiten stehen noch auf der Roadmap im Wiki, unter \"Immediate\", und wurden nie gebaut. Die Maschinerie, die sie skizzieren, ist nicht die, die diese Serie baut (ein defc mit Modus-Deklarationen, Set-Domänen wie die FD-Domänen), aber ihre Testfälle sind die, die sie beantwortet. Sie legt auch die Messlatte. Eine Relation, die nur in manche Richtungen funktioniert, ist eine Funktion mit Extraschritten. Deshalb hält die Serie jede Relation an eine Regel: Sie antwortet in jedem Modus korrekt, oder sie wartet, bis sie es kann (Teil 2). Relationale Maps: ein Rundgang durch die API. Kein nicht unterstütztes Verhalten: warum das Design neu gemacht werden musste, und wie unbekannte Keys, lazy Checks und suspendierte Goals zusammenpassen. Schnell machen: von 33 s auf 8 ms, und was langsam war. Vektoren: offene Vektoren, Längen exakt gelöst, und get-in als Abfragesprache. Literatur und Parität mit miniKanren: wo das in der Forschung steht, und was core.logic noch fehlt. Auf dem Weg zu relationalem hyperdimensionalem Rechnen: dieselben Datenstrukturen, als Hypervektoren kodiert. Sets: Maps ohne Werte, und was das bringt. Seite an Seite mit miniKanren: die Testsuite von faster-miniKanren auf core.logic, die Bugs, die sie gefunden hat, und Maps, Sets und Vektoren neben ihren Kodierungen in miniKanren. Ein relationaler Clojure-Interpreter: evalo für eine Teilmenge von Clojure, mit relationalen Maps als Umgebung; was er rückwärts kann, und wo seine Suche noch langsam ist. Als Nächstes vielleicht: Records als Typen, und ClojureScript. Siehe Future Work. Generiert von Claude im Auftrag von Benjamin Schwerdtner. Datum: 2026-10-28 Wed 00:00 Email: Benjamin.Schwerdtner@gmail.com About Contact"}, :excerpts [{:text "... searchbar. Work With Benjamin Hire me Resume CV Claude Code Programming with Claude Code Explore! Get a random piece of Bennis mind!...", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :content}]}]